# Logging & Traceability

Every module must be traceable through stdlib `logging` — no `print`, no silent
control flow. This is required for new modules and when touching existing ones.

## Baseline

- Top of each module: `import logging` then `log = logging.getLogger(__name__)`.
- Never configure handlers/levels in library code (only the app/CLI entrypoint does).
- Use `%`-style lazy args: `log.info("x=%s", x)` — not f-strings in the log call.

## What to log (traceability, not spam)

- **Decisions & branches**: which path/method was taken and why (e.g. allocation
  method chosen, `optimal` vs `confidence` combination, block vs approve).
- **Fallbacks/degradations**: any silent fallback must log at `debug`/`warning`
  (e.g. Kelly → conviction weighting, optimal → confidence mean, live calendar →
  bundled CSV). Never swallow an exception with a bare `pass`.
- **External I/O & failures**: network/file loads and their outcome; log caught
  exceptions with context (`log.warning("... (%s)", exc)` or `log.exception(...)`).
- **Safety-critical events** at `warning`/`error`: execution-gate blocks,
  news-guard blackouts, kill-switch trips, risk-limit breaches. Include the
  audit id / symbol / cycle where available.
- **Entry/summary** of expensive computations at `debug`/`info` (inputs sized,
  key outputs), e.g. overfitting verdict, Monte Carlo sim counts, tear-sheet render.

## Levels

- `debug`: routine internals, per-item decisions, fallbacks in hot loops.
- `info`: notable state (engine/config changes, renders, calendar source loaded).
- `warning`: recoverable problems, blocked/degraded behaviour.
- `error`/`exception`: failures; use `log.exception` inside `except` to keep the traceback.

## Reference implementations

`eval/overfitting.py`, `eval/robustness.py`, `eval/tearsheet.py`,
`live/news_guard.py`, `live/execution_safety.py` (also writes an immutable audit
JSONL), `agents/research/_combine.py`, `agents/trader.py` (Kelly fallback).
